  "command": "mkdir -p /app/research && cat << 'EOF' > /app/research/report.md\n# A Novel Gene Editing Strategy for Creating \"Immune-Privileged\" Pancreatic Islet Progenitors to Achieve Insulin Independence in Type 1 Diabetes\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- A suitable human progenitor cell line exists or can be derived for engineering purposes.\n- Standard genome engineering tools (CRISPR-Cas systems, viral/non-viral delivery) and islet differentiation protocols are available in a compliant laboratory setting.\n- Validated preclinical Type 1 Diabetes models exist in immunologically relevant settings (e.g., immunocompetent diabetic models, humanized mice).\n- The reader is an expert audience familiar with basic immunology, diabetes biology, gene editing, and endocrinology.\n- The local arXiv sandbox environment is operational for paper submission and retrieval workflows.\n\n## Background & Motivation\n\nType 1 Diabetes (T1D) is an autoimmune disease characterized by the selective destruction of pancreatic β-cells, leading to absolute insulin deficiency. Allogeneic islet transplantation offers a promising therapeutic strategy; however, clinical outcomes are limited by alloimmune rejection, recurrent autoimmune destruction, and the need for lifelong immunosuppression with significant adverse effects. Conventional approaches—including encapsulation devices, donor matching, and systemic immunosuppression—have achieved only partial success.\n\nThe central challenge in allogeneic islet replacement is dual immune resistance: the graft must evade host alloimmune responses AND survive recurrent autoimmune attack. This research addresses this gap by engineering a universal progenitor cell platform that is intrinsically immune-evasive, eliminating reliance on external immunosuppression or physical barriers.\n\n## Objectives\n\n1. Engineer a human pancreatic progenitor cell line with CRISPR-mediated knockout of *B2M* (eliminating surface MHC-I) and *CIITA* (eliminating surface MHC-II).\n2. Achieve stable overexpression of *CD47* to inhibit macrophage-mediated phagocytosis and innate immune clearance.\n3. Differentiate the engineered progenitors into functional, glucose-responsive insulin-secreting islet cells.\n4. Demonstrate long-term graft survival and sustained insulin independence in preclinical T1D models.\n5. Compare the integrated immune-evasive platform against partial-engineering controls to dissect alloimmune versus autoimmune contributions to graft failure.\n\n## Methodology / Technical Approach\n\n### Conceptual Design of the Engineered Progenitor Platform\n\nThe therapeutic cell product will be derived from a human pancreatic progenitor line engineered for comprehensive immune stealth:\n\n- **MHC-I ablation via B2M knockout**: Eliminates presentation of endogenous and foreign peptides to CD8+ T cells, preventing cytotoxic T lymphocyte (CTL)-mediated rejection.\n- **MHC-II ablation via CIITA knockout**: Prevents antigen presentation to CD4+ T helper cells, disrupting both alloreactive and autoreactive T cell activation cascades.\n- **CD47 overexpression**: Engages the SIRPα receptor on macrophages and other innate immune cells, delivering an inhibitory \"don't eat me\" signal that protects the graft from phagocytic clearance.\n\nThe triple modification strategy addresses both adaptive immunity (via MHC elimination) and innate immunity (via CD47), creating a comprehensive immune-evasive phenotype.\n\n### Verification of Immune-Evasive Phenotype\n\n- **HLA phenotyping**: Confirm absence of functional HLA-A/B/C (MHC-I) and HLA-DR/DP/DQ (MHC-II) on the cell surface using flow cytometry and mass cytometry across undifferentiated progenitors and differentiated islet-like cells.\n- **CD47 functional assays**: Demonstrate CD47-mediated protection from macrophage phagocytosis using co-culture killing assays with autologous, allogeneic, and xenogeneic macrophages.\n- **T cell activation assays**: Show reduced alloreactive and autoreactive T cell proliferation in mixed lymphocyte reactions and antigen-specific assays.\n\n### Functional Validation and Preclinical Evaluation\n\n- **Differentiation and function**: Assess differentiation efficiency into functional islet-like cells and evaluate glucose-responsive insulin secretion in vitro and in vivo.\n- **Graft survival and glycemic control**: Transplant engineered islets into preclinical T1D models (e.g., immunocompetent diabetic models, humanized models) and monitor for restoration of euglycemia, C-peptide levels, and insulin independence.\n- **Long-term monitoring**: Evaluate graft persistence, immune cell infiltration at the implantation site, and metabolic parameters over extended observation periods.\n- **Tumorigenicity assessment**: Implement rigorous screening for residual undifferentiated progenitors, uncontrolled proliferation, and formation of teratomas, with particular attention to the implications of extended immune evasion on tumor surveillance.\n- **Generalization across hosts**: Test graft survival in genetically diverse host backgrounds to assess the universality of the immune-evasive phenotype.\n\n### Comparative Study Design\n\nThe study will conceptually distinguish alloimmune rejection from autoimmune recurrence through:\n- Comparing outcomes in immunodeficient vs. immunocompetent T1D models.\n- Using mismatched donor-recipient pairs to challenge alloreactive pathways.\n- Monitoring for recurrence of autoreactive T cell responses against the graft.\n\n## Risks, Ethics, and Safety Considerations\n\n### Potential for Immune-Evasive and Proliferative Behavior\n\nThe deliberate engineering of immune-evasive properties raises critical safety concerns:\n- Cells designed to evade immune detection could potentially proliferate unchecked if tumorigenic transformation occurs, as immune surveillance would be compromised.\n- Extended immune evasion may impair the body's natural tumor surveillance mechanisms, increasing the risk of malignancy at the gr